/-! # Smoothness of a torus fixed scheme

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Theorem 13.1, printed p.254,
and Lemma 13.6 and Theorem 13.7, printed p.256
(blueprint: lem-sfb-torus-fixed-scheme-smooth).

The representing condition is stated on all algebra-valued points, with fixedness
required after every algebra extension. The action is the existing internal action
object, and the torus predicate uses the recorded algebraic-closure base change.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory Opposite

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A closed scheme representing the fixed subfunctor of a torus acting on a smooth
finite-type affine scheme is smooth over the field.

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Theorem 13.1, printed p.254,
with proof through Lemma 13.6 and Theorem 13.7, printed p.256
(blueprint: lem-sfb-torus-fixed-scheme-smooth).

Proof:
1. Write X = Spec B and F = Spec (B/I). The quotient is finite type and, over
   the Noetherian field, finitely presented by
   lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-noetherian-finite-presentation. The fixed-functor
   condition commutes with field extension, by its quantifier over all algebras.
2. Use lem-sfb-scheme-large-algclosed-jacobson to extend the algebraic closure
   to an algebraically closed field K where all closed points of F_K are rational.
   The torus splits there. Use lem-pc-field-extension-flat,
   lem-sfb-scheme-lfp-smooth-flat-base-change and
   lem-weil-pbc-smooth-local-on-source to obtain smoothness of X_K, then
   lem-weil-pbc-smooth-scheme-over-field-regular to obtain regular local rings.
3. At a closed fixed point x, lem-sfb-torus-fixed-local-ideal-jets identifies
   the local ring of F_K with A/J, where A is the ambient regular local ring
   and J is generated by differences t*f-f. It supplies the rational actions
   on the finite jets and on the cotangent space V.
4. Apply lem-sfb-gm-representation-weight-basis successively to the commuting
   factors of the split torus. Choose a simultaneous weight basis of V, with
   its s nonzero weights first. For every n, lift each vector to its own weight
   component in the finite jet and then to an element of the maximal ideal.
5. These lifts generate A/m^(n+1) as an algebra by successive approximation.
   Each nonzero character takes a value other than 1 over the infinite field K,
   so its weight vector lies in the difference ideal. After killing these
   vectors the remaining generators have weight zero, so every difference
   vanishes. Thus J+m^(n+1) equals the ideal of the first s lifts modulo m^(n+1).
6. The integer s does not depend on n. Apply
   lem-sfb-regular-quotient-parameter-approximation to obtain regularity of A/J,
   including s=0 and s=dim V.
7. Use lem-sfb-regular-separable-affine-smooth-neighbourhood at each rational
   closed point of F_K. Its maximal smooth open exists by
   lem-sfb-space-maximal-smooth-open and contains every closed point. The
   Jacobson property forces the closed complement to be empty.
8. The extension K/k is flat and surjective on spectra by
   lem-pc-field-extension-flat and lem-pc-field-extension-surjective, and
   lem-weil-pbc-surjective-base-change makes F_K to F surjective. Descend
   pointwise smoothness using lem-sfb-scheme-lfp-smooth-flat-base-change and
   the finite presentation from step 1, then use
   lem-weil-pbc-smooth-local-on-source to conclude Smooth F.hom. -/
theorem torus_fixed_scheme_smooth {k : Type u} [Field k]
    (T : Grp (Over (Spec (.of k)))) [IsAffine T.X.left]
    [LocallyOfFiniteType T.X.hom] [QuasiCompact T.X.hom]
    (hT : MultiplicativeType.IsTorus T)
    (X : Over (Spec (.of k))) [IsAffine X.left]
    [LocallyOfFiniteType X.hom] [QuasiCompact X.hom] [Smooth X.hom]
    [action : ModObj T.X X]
    (F : Over (Spec (.of k))) (i : F ⟶ X) [IsClosedImmersion i.left]
    (hfixed : ∀ (A : CommAlgCat.{u} k)
      (x : (algSpec (.of k)).obj (op A) ⟶ X),
      (∃ z : (algSpec (.of k)).obj (op A) ⟶ F, z ≫ i = x) ↔
        ∀ (A' : CommAlgCat.{u} k) (f : A ⟶ A')
          (t : (algSpec (.of k)).obj (op A') ⟶ T.X),
          t • ((algSpec (.of k)).map f.op ≫ x) = (algSpec (.of k)).map f.op ≫ x) :
    Smooth F.hom := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
